On Jun 23, 10:16 am, scott.dy...@sbcglobal.net wrote:
> I just reached my very first ascension (Enki the Samurai), and was
> wondering: how common it is for a samurai to develop reliable
> spellcasting abilities?

Not very, I'd say.  I've gotten a Valk to be pretty reliable
(they're similar to Samurai in this regard), but it took
everything possible to help: a +5 HoB, robe, gauntlets of
dexterity (don't know if they change much), max'ed out
attributes and skill.  Anything less, and even force bolt won't
be at 0% failure.  And even with all that, touch of death
was still at about 14% failure.  Which limits its application;
most of the time, when you'd use it, it's because you need a
100% reliable insta-kill.

In practice, even when the Valk had most at 0% failure, I didn't
use them much.  Cure sickness, when needed.  Maybe cone of cold,
if there's water around that needs to be frozen, or occasionally
for mind flayers and the like (but daggers, or arrows in the
case of a Samurai, are usually just as good).  But that's about
it.

> I WoW'd a helm of brilliance, ate a LOT of
> illithids, quaffed a couple !oGA, all of which left me with more
> intelligence than my wisdom knew what to do with ;-). A pudding farm
> blessed me with stacks and stacks of spellbooks (courtesy of my god,
> of course), most of which fell within my two advanceable magic skill
> slots (attack and clerical), and I managed to get to a 30% failure
> rate on virtually all my known spells. Is this exceptional for a
> samurai, or a joke? Just wondering.

Some points you don't mention: no metalic armour (but I imagine
you had dragon scales anyway, which are OK), no metalic gloves
or boots (no gauntlets of power or iron boots---but dexterity is
OK, as are speed boots or jumping boots), and above all, a robe.
Wearing a robe makes an enormous difference.

Note that a robe means gray dragon scales, which means
reflection from some other source.  For Samurai, Valks and
Knights, twoweaponing is de rigor, that only leaves the amulet.
The result is that you don't have much choice to adapt to what
you actually get.  In practice, my two ascensions were a Valk
and a Knight, and neither ever had a robe, nor could cast
reliably.  (In the case of the Knight, it's a real bummer, since
not being able to cast magic missle makes his quest artifact
much less interesting.   If I had it to do again, I'd simply
leave it somewhere, and save the weight, like I always do with
the Samurai quest artifact.)

